  "title": "Doppelganger",
  "summary": "In 1995, I was in a relationship with a woman from Singapore who lived in Australia. I would wake/stay up for our 3 a.m. chats, all over IRC via 28K baud modem. I came to realize that the self I was sharing with someone on the other side of the world was perhaps one of the emotionally truest versions of who I was. At the time I didn’t understand how to share my deepest thoughts and dreams with the people I lived with, or those I considered my best friends. Yet in this world of simple text and private virtual rooms, I was more fully “real.”\r\n\r\nSometimes I wonder if those strings of text still exist on a server somewhere, an authentic 20-year-old me trying to understand and share a self, as translated by computers screaming bits over phone lines. Could I be reconstructed from that database?\r\n\r\nAlmost three decades later, we have all been feeding fragments of ourselves into hundreds of databases that are farmed and cross-fertilized. They produce digital clones representing us in every important digital transaction and communication. As I was developing this show in mid-2021, we were just starting to imagine how to resume a more physical presence at work and school, and in our zoom hangovers wondering: Are our digital selves more important than our physical selves? How do our selves engage differently in our current world and culture? What ways have our assets and labor become digital only? Would you rather have someone hit you in the face or hurt your credit score?\r\n\r\nYour digital self is also increasingly the one who “owns” your access to music, movies, event tickets, games and more, as our digital assets become our primary assets. David Rudnick started <a href=\"https://twitter.com/David_Rudnick/status/1372157141096787976?s=20&t=gWwUEb4TgFuIXjO9wShCMA\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">describing</a> some of these issues as an existential struggle for primacy where, “Physical prime: an ideology in which value + meaning only exist to the extent they can be traced to a ph...",
